.Dd 2025-04-14
.Dt PIMSYNC 1
.\" TODO: replace the following line with the version at build time.
.Os PIMSYNC
.Sh NAME
.Nm pimsync
.Nd synchronise caldav, carddav, webcal and local directories
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar configfile
.Op Fl v Ar loglevel
.Ar command
.Op Ar pair…
.Nm
.Ic version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
synchronizes calendars and contacts. For calendars, it supports operating on a
local directories, CalDAV servers or Webcal URLs. For contacts, it supports
operating on local directories or CardDav servers.
.Pp
Glossary of relevant terminology:
.Bl -tag
.It Sy item
A single event, todo, journal entry or contact.
.It Sy collection
Grouping of multiple items. Can be one of (1) a single calendar which contains
events, todos and/or journal entries, or (2) an address book which contains
contacts.
.It Sy storage
Location which contains multiple collections. For example: an account on a
CalDAV server, or a local directory with child directories, each representing a
calendar.
See
.Xr pimsync.conf 5
for details on supported storage implementations and their configuration.
.It Sy pair
Two storages which are configured to synchronise with each other. A single
storage may form part of more than one pair.
.It Sy conflict
When synchronising two storages, if a single item has been modified on both
sides but has different content on both sides, that item is said to be in
conflict.
.It Sy property
Metadata attribute which applies to a collection. Examples: displayname,
colour, order.
.It Sy resource
An item or a collection. This term is mostly used internally and in logs.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar configfile
Read the configuration file from the specified path, instead of the default.
See
.Sx FILES
below and
.Xr pimsync.conf 5 .
.It Fl v Ar loglevel
Set logger to
.Ar loglevel ,
which must be one of
.Cm trace ,
.Cm debug ,
.Cm info ,
.Cm warn or
.Cm error .
The default is
.Cm warn .
When specifying
.Cm trace ,
logs may contain sensitive data, see
.Sx SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
below.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic check Op Ar pair…
Check configuration and exit. Validates the configuration itself and ensures
that the storage is valid. E.g.: a
.\" FIXME: .Cm might not be correct here.
.Cm vdir
must be an existing
directory, and
.Cm caldav
server must report CalDAV capabilities, etc.
.It Ic daemon Op Ar pair…
Continuously keep storages in sync. The
.Fl r Ar READY_FD
argument may be used to provide a readiness notification file descriptor.
.Pp
Once
.Nm
has reached its ready state, the daemon will monitor storages for changes and
synchronise them. In case of non-fatal errors, pimsync will continue running
the background and retry failed operations later.
.Pp
Individual items that fail to sync will be skipped.
.It Ic discover Op Ar pair…
Discover and print collections. This command merely provides information, and
does in any way alter pimsync's internal state.
.It Ic resolve-conflicts Op Ar pair…
Manually resolve conflicts between storages.
.Pp
Execute an external program, specified in the configuration file, which
performs conflict resolution program for each conflicting item.
.It Ic sync Op Fl n Op Fl i Op Ar pair…
Synchronise storages once and exit.
The
.Fl n
flag performs a dry run and won't perform any write operations.
The
.Fl i
flag enables interactive mode, prompting to confirm the plan before proceeding
with the synchronisation.
.It Ic repair Op Ar storage…
Repair invalid items. Currently, this adds missing
.Va UID
fields, but other fixes are likely to be included in future. Repairing both
collections from a pair is highly discouraged; repair on storage and then
synchronise the pair instead.
.It Ic version
Print version information and exit.
.El
.Pp
If a
.Ar pair
is specified, only operate on pair(s) with the given name(s).
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If defined, search for the configuration file relative to this
directory. See
.Sx FILES
below.
.El
.Sh FILES
If
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is defined, the configuration shall be read from:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pimsync/pimsync.conf
.Ed
.Pp
If the above is not defined, the configuration file shall be read from:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
~/.config/pimsync/pimsync.conf
.Ed
.Pp
See
.Xr pimsync.conf 5
for details on the configuration file format.
.Sh READINESS NOTIFICATION
Readiness notification allows a service manager to monitor when pimsync has
successfully started. It is considered to have successfully started when the
configuration file has been parsed and all credentials have been resolved. At
this point, no further human interaction shall be required.
.Pp
Once the service is ready, it will write
.Ql READY=1
followed by a newline
character to the file descriptor
.Ar READY_FD ,
and then close it. This is compatible with the readiness notification mechanism
implemented by s6, dinit and systemd.
.Pp
See: https://skarnet.org/software/s6/notifywhenup.html
.Sh PRIVACY
The only network communications shall be with the servers for configured
storages, and the locally configured DNS resolver.
.Pp
The status database file contains metadata of configured storages, their
collections and items.
.Sh DATA VALIDATION
Data synchronised is not validated. This is an intentional design choice: if
your calendar application produces events that are technically invalid, pimsync
will synchronise, and you will be able to access the exact same data on other
hosts. You may also need to synchronise invalid data in order to fix the
invalidity.
.Pp
In order to map items between storage, pimsync does some minor parsing of items
to extract their UID. Items must be syntactically valid components, even if
semantically invalid.
.Pp
In order to interact with CalDAV/CardDAV resources where a UID cannot be
extracted, use the
.Ic repair
command to fix invalid entries, or consider using
.Xr davcli 1 .
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
When service discovery is enabled, the real hostname and port of the DAV server
is resolved using DNS records. The system's DNS resolver is used. It is
expected that the system resolver performs DNSSEC validation and will not
return invalid results. Pimsync does not perform DNSSEC validation itself.
.Pp
Pimsync will follow symlinks when reading items. Care should be taken when a
user has write permission to a storage, but another user runs pimsync on that
same storage. This may change at a future date.
.Pp
Items across storages are considered the same if they share the same UID. An
actor who can write a single item into a storage on either side can potentially
trigger an overwrite of another item on the other storage. An actor who can
write to a single entry should be considered to have access to manipulate the
entire storage.
.Pp
Pimsync will retain credentials in memory during its entire lifetime. Any actor
with read access to pimsync's memory space may extract secret credentials from
it. Likewise, core dumps from pimsync should not be shared with untrusted
parties.
.Pp
When logging with
.Fl v
.Cm trace ,
logs MAY contain credentials, secrets, data inside of storage items and other
sensitive information.
.Sh SYNCHRONISATION ALGORITHM
After pimsync synchronises two storages, it will store metadata from the items
that it has synchronised into its internal status database. During future
synchronisation processes, if the items on both storages don't match, the
status database is used to determine which side has changed and which side
remains the same as last time. This information is then used to plan which of
the item should be kept and which one should be overwritten. If both items have
changed, they are said to be in conflict, and the conflict must be resolved
manually (See
.Sx CONFLICT RESOLUTION
above).
.Pp
The status database associates item metadata with the pair of collections that
were being synchronised with each other (called a "mapping"). If the
configuration changes, and different collections are being synchronised with
each other, then the status data for their items no longer applies and is
ignored. This prevents using accidentally using the wrong data when mappings
have been reconfigured.
.Pp
The algorithm is closely based on the original one for vdirsyncer(1). Further
details are available at https://unterwaditzer.net/2016/sync-algorithm.html.
.Pp
Deleting the status database will force
.Nm
to synchronise both storages as if it were the first time it's ever seen both
of them. Items that exist and are identical on both sides shall be fine, but
items which are not identical will be considered in conflict (since
.Nm
cannot unambiguously decide which one to keep).
.Sh PROTECTIONS
When all items in a collection are deleted, pimsync(1) will assume a user
error, and refuse to operate in this collection. This behaviour can be disabled
with the
.Cm on_empty
directive. See
.Xr pimsync.conf 5
for further details.
.Sh CAVEATS
For storages of type
.Cm vdir ,
checking whether an item was modified by an external process is subject to race
conditions. Pimsync will first check that an item has not been modified, and
then update it. If an external program updates the same item between the check
and the modification, those changes will be lost. This issue is due to
limitations in POSIX filesystem primitive. It is unlikely that it can be
resolved.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bl -tag
.It Run daemon with verbose logging:
.D1 pimsync -v info daemon
.It Interactive conflict resolution for pair named Ql contacts :
.D1 pimsync resolve-conflicts contacts
.It Show version and exit:
.D1 pimsync version
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pimsync.conf 5 ,
